ies (cycle order, idea numbering, combined score)\",\n438\t      \"queries phrased as 'effect of model size/width on grokking delay' or 'scaling of grokking with model size' (risk of surfacing the excluded prior paper; the starter literature plus component literatures suffice)\",\n439\t      \"internal harness terms: eligible, rubric, worker, program_1\",\n440\t      \"LLM scaling-law or large-language-model pretraining queries not tied to memorization capacity\"\n441\t    ],\n442\t    \"queries\": [\n443\t      {\n444\t        \"category\": \"core_method\",\n445\t        \"query\": \"grokking generalization beyond overfitting small algorithmic datasets modular arithmetic\",\n446\t        \"rationale\": \"Anchors the delayed-generalization phenomenon and the modular-arithmetic task family used in every run.\"\n447\t      },\n448\t      {\n449\t        \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n450\t        \"query\": \"progress measures for grokking mechanistic interpretability modular addition transformer\",\n451\t        \"rationale\": \"Defines the small one-block transformer / modular-addition setting and Fourier-circuit view that the width sweeps build on.\"\n452\t      },\n453\t      {\n454\t        \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n455\t        \"query\": \"grokking circuit efficiency memorization versus generalization circuits\",\n456\t        \"rationale\": \"Circuit-efficiency explanation is the main competing account to the memorization-capacity crossover hypothesis (idea 1).\"\n457\t      },\n458\t      {\n459\t        \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n460\t        \"query\": \"grokking transition from lazy to rich training dynamics\",\n461\t        \"rationale\": \"Lazy-to-rich account is the comparator behind the width-aware output-scaling variant (idea 2).\"\n462\t      },\n463\t      {\n464\t        \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n465\t        \"query\": \"Omnigrok loss landscape weight norm grokking\",\n466\t        \"rationale\": \"Weight-norm / loss-landscape explanation and initialization-scale dependence relevant to init-scale and weight-decay choices.\"\n467\t      },\n468\t      {\n469\t        \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n470\t        \"query\": \"unifying grokking and double descent model size\",\n471\t        \"rationale\": \"Connects grokking to double descent in model size and data fraction; relevant to the width-by-train-fraction sweep.\"\n472\t      },\n473\t      {\n474\t        \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n475\t        \"query\": \"maximal update parametrization width-independent feature learning hyperparameter transfer\",\n476\t        \"rationale\": \"Idea 2 introduced muP-like width-aware output scaling and idea 3 a W=m*V hidden-layer reparametrization; muP is the literature behind both.\"\n477\t      },\n478\t      {\n479\t        \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n480\t        \"query\": \"effective learning rate width scaling normalization weight decay AdamW\",\n481\t        \"rationale\": \"Idea 3 tests an effective-learning-rate confound in width; decoupled weight decay (AdamW) and effective-LR dynamics are needed to interpret it.\"\n482\t      },\n483\t      {\n484\t        \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n485\t        \"query\": \"memorization capacity of models bits per parameter\",\n486\t        \"rationale\": \"Memorization-capacity crossover hypothesis (t_mem falls with width, critical width scales with train fraction) needs capacity-estimation and memorization-dynamics work.\"\n487\t      }\n488\t    ],\n489\t    \"result_driven_themes\": [\n490\t      \"final sealed evaluation: fitted log-delay vs width slope -0.020 with bootstrap CI [-0.58, 0.35] spanning zero, vs reference slope -0.37; 40% of runs censored (no grokking within budget) -> conditional/inconclusive width effect\",\n491\t      \"held-out controls (shuffled labels) stay near chance (max val acc ~0.012), so detected generalization is genuine\",\n492\t      \"effective-l