{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HdxWLMhbkkq59W9Ejds8Kt","type":"tool_result","content":"340\t32 & 18{,}784 & 2.00 \\\\\n341\t64 & 62{,}144 & 1.41 \\\\\n342\t128 & 222{,}592 & 1.00 \\\\\n343\t256 & 838{,}400 & 0.71 \\\\\n344\t\\bottomrule\n345\t\\end{tabular}\n346\t\\end{table}\n347\t\n348\t\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n349\t\\label{sec:si-dev}\n350\tFigure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. The rank order by score is reversed relative to the end-of-training accuracy: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), so the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$; the retained records do not show whether the censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. The lower V3 seed ($0.801$) lies just above the V1 mean ($0.798$), while the higher V3 seed ($0.899$) lies above the V2 mean ($0.828$). With two seeds per configuration, the seed-to-seed spreads ($0.051$, $0.043$, $0.098$) are no smaller than the differences between configuration means ($0.031$, $0.022$), so no ordering of the configurations is supported.\n351\t% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant1_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant2_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant3_metrics.json]]\n352\t\n353\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n354\t\\centering\n355\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variants_descriptive.pdf}\n356\t\\caption{Development-plane comparison of three configurations (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, two seeds; not the sealed evaluation). (a) Per-seed and mean held-out-accuracy AUC. (b) Number of the six runs censored at the step limit.}\n357\t\\label{fig:dev}\n358\t\\end{figure}\n359\t\n360\t\\begin{table}[h]\n361\t\\centering\n362\t\\caption{Development-plane summary (two seeds $\\times$ three widths $=6$ runs per configuration).}\n363\t\\label{tab:si-dev}\n364\t\\small\n365\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n366\t\\toprule\n367\tConfiguration & Score & Mean final held-out acc. & Censored runs & Control max acc. \\\\\n368\t\\midrule\n369\tV1 & $0.798$ & $0.981$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\n370\tV2 & $0.828$ & $0.944$ & $6/6$ & $0.022$ \\\\\n371\tV3 (evaluated) & $0.850$ & $0.921$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\n372\t\\bottomrule\n373\t\\end{tabular}\n374\t\\end{table}\n375\t\n376\t\\subsection{Analyses and hypotheses not performed}\n377\t\\label{sec:si-untested}\n378\tThe following were considered during development and have \\emph{no} results in the artifacts: a capacity-crossover account in which fitting time falls faster with width than generalization time (separate fitting and generalization steps are not retained); a critical width as a function of training fraction; a sweep of the hidden-layer exponent; a sweep of the output-scaling exponent and unembedding scale; an intervention on weight decay; and any weight-norm or circuit-level diagnostics. Nothing in the main text depends on them.\n379\t\n380\t\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n381\t\\label{sec:si-repro}\n382\tThe accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}, which computes every derived number in Tables~\\ref{tab:resolution} and~\\ref{tab:si-model} and the text from the evaluator records and writes \\texttt{derived/resolution.json}; and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py}, which produces Figures~\\ref{fig:slope} and~\\ref{fig:dev} from that file. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Data are generated in memory from the modulus and seed, with no download. The aggregate metrics (slope, interval, reference slope, censored fraction, control, score) are those recorded by the evaluator. Re-running the evaluator requires the evaluated model's dependencies (PyTorch and NumPy); the sealed evaluation took about $3.3$ hours of wall-clock time on the recorded hardware, whose specification is not retained in the artifacts.\n383\t\n384\t\\subsection{LLM usage}\n385\t\\label{sec:si-llm}\n386\tLarge language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the workload manifests, and the decision rule for the width-slope test; the models did not edit them. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.\n387\t\n388\t\\end{document}\n389\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ce8591f-0a56-4848-9009-c3a2c9063cb6","uuid":"04ac914a-89b6-4651-b0df-166fe4c1825a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:50:25.300Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"32 & 18{,}784 & 2.00 \\\\\n64 & 62{,}144 & 1.41 \\\\\n128 & 222{,}592 & 1.00 \\\\\n256 & 838{,}400 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n\\label{sec:si-dev}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. The rank order by score is reversed relative to the end-of-training accuracy: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), so the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$; the retained records do not show whether the censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. The lower V3 seed ($0.801$) lies just above the V1 mean ($0.798$), while the higher V3 seed ($0.899$) lies above the V2 mean ($0.828$). With two seeds per configuration, the seed-to-seed spreads ($0.051$, $0.043$, $0.098$) are no smaller than the differences between configuration means ($0.031$, $0.022$), so no ordering of the configurations is supported.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant1_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant2_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant3_metrics.json]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variants_descriptive.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-plane comparison of three configurations (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, two seeds; not the sealed evaluation). (a) Per-seed and mean held-out-accuracy AUC. (b) Number of the six runs censored at the step limit.}\n\\label{fig:dev}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-plane summary (two seeds $\\times$ three widths $=6$ runs per configuration).}\n\\label{tab:si-dev}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Mean final held-out acc. & Censored runs & Control max acc. \\\\\n\\midrule\nV1 & $0.798$ & $0.981$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\nV2 & $0.828$ & $0.944$ & $6/6$ & $0.022$ \\\\\nV3 (evaluated) & $0.850$ & $0.921$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Analyses and hypotheses not performed}\n\\label{sec:si-untested}\nThe following were considered during development and have \\emph{no} results in the artifacts: a capacity-crossover account in which fitting time falls faster with width than generalization time (separate fitting and generalization steps are not retained); a critical width as a function of training fraction; a sweep of the hidden-layer exponent; a sweep of the output-scaling exponent and unembedding scale; an intervention on weight decay; and any weight-norm or circuit-level diagnostics. Nothing in the main text depends on them.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n\\label{sec:si-repro}\nThe accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}, which computes every derived number in Tables~\\ref{tab:resolution} and~\\ref{tab:si-model} and the text from the evaluator records and writes \\texttt{derived/resolution.json}; and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py}, which produces Figures~\\ref{fig:slope} and~\\ref{fig:dev} from that file. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Data are generated in memory from the modulus and seed, with no download. The aggregate metrics (slope, interval, reference slope, censored fraction, control, score) are those recorded by the evaluator. Re-running the evaluator requires the evaluated model's dependencies (PyTorch and NumPy); the sealed evaluation took about $3.3$ hours of wall-clock time on the recorded hardware, whose specification is not retained in the artifacts.\n\n\\subsection{LLM usage}\n\\label{sec:si-llm}\nLarge language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the workload manifests, and the decision rule for the width-slope test; the models did not edit them. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":50,"startLine":340,"totalLines":389}}}
