{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ERJVrqwf43Nas8YHPqzhcF","type":"tool_result","content":"# Agentic evidence audit\n\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n\n## Summary\nThe numbers and qualification handling match the final evaluation record, but the abstract overclaims calibration and an unsupported literature gap, and the development-plane wording is looser than the data.\n\n## Findings\n### 1. abstract-calibrated-overclaim — Abstract\n- claim: The evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a calibrated non-answer rather than a failure\n- issue: Nothing in the evidence supports \"calibrated\". The Method section says the plain percentile interval from four clusters (at most 35 distinct resamples) makes no claim of nominal coverage and may not be nominal, and that the interval is only a coarse summary. Qualification (qualified, completed, correct=true) supports validity of the measurement, not calibration of the interval.\n- evidence: paper.tex Method \"Caveat on few clusters\" and \"A coarse bootstrap\" paragraphs; claims_grounding.json claim[0] (qualification is integrity of measurement only) and claim[14] (35 multisets); paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics have no coverage or calibration diagnostic.\n- requested_fix: Replace \"calibrated non-answer\" with a plain statement such as \"a valid but inconclusive measurement\", and keep the existing note that the interval's coverage is not guaranteed.\n\n### 2. novelty-gap-ungrounded — Abstract and Related Work\n- claim: its dependence on model size has received little systematic measurement (Abstract); none of them has ... measured delay against width under a fixed protocol (Related Work)\n- issue: The cited sources cover grokking accounts, double descent, and memorization, but no claim or source in claims_grounding.json supports a statement about how much the delay-versus-width dependence has been measured. The abstract states this unhedged, while Related Work hedges it as a non-exhaustive reading. The retrieval plan also flagged a possible prior width-and-grokking-delay paper that was excluded. Memorization capacity is likewise listed as an account of grokking in the abstract without a citation for that role.\n- evidence: claims_grounding.json claims[21]-[32] only ground what each cited paper says; references_provenance.json reference_query_plan forbidden_query_patterns note a possible prior paper on width and grokking delay; paper.tex Abstract sentence 1 versus Related Work \"Grokking and its explanations\".\n- requested_fix: Delete the \"little systematic measurement\" claim from the abstract, or qualify it as \"to our non-exhaustive reading\", matching Related Work. Do not present memorization capacity as a cited account of grokking.\n\n### 3. dev-plane-wording-1 — Results (Validity checks and development-plane context) and Supplementary Information (Development-plane comparison)\n- claim: per-seed scores of V3 (0.801, 0.899) ... lie just above the V1 mean (0.798); differences between them are of the same size as the spread between the two seeds\n- issue: Only the 0.801 seed is just above the V1 mean of 0.798. The other V3 seed (0.899) is about 0.10 above it. The configuration-mean difference V1 to V2 is 0.0308, which rounds to 0.031, not the 0.030 printed. The main text says the differences are \"of the same size\" as the seed spreads, but the seed spreads (0.051, 0.043, 0.098) are 1.7 to 4.5 times the mean differences (0.031, 0.022). The grounding entry cites only result.improvement_001 to 003, which carry scores only. The censoring counts, final accuracies, controls, and per-seed scores come from source_evidence/search_variants and derived/resolution.json.\n- evidence: derived/resolution.json search.variants (seed_scores V1 0.772/0.823, V2 0.807/0.850, V3 0.801/0.899; scores 0.79769, 0.82847, 0.85015); paper_data.json grounding_sources result.improvement_001 to 003 (score values only); paper.tex SI section sec:si-dev and the Results development-plane paragraph; claims_grounding.json claim[18].\n- requested_fix: Rewrite to say the lower V3 seed (0.801) lies just above the V1 mean while the higher seed (0.899) lies above the V2 mean. State the first mean difference as 0.031. Change \"same size\" to \"no larger than the seed-to-seed spreads\". Add the search_variants and derived/resolution.json evidence refs to the grounding entry for the censoring, accuracy, and control values.\npaper_strategy.md:9:In a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW on modular addition (p=97, train fraction 0.4, weight decay 1.0, full batch, 10,000-step limit), does the delay between fitting the training set and generalizing decrease with width, as a pre-declared seed-cluster slope test can detect?\npaper_strategy.md:12:The sealed final evaluation (outcome `qualified`, status completed, correct true; 4 seeds x 5 widths 16-256) measured a ln(delay)-vs-log2(width) slope of -0.020 with 95% seed-cluster bootstrap CI [-0.585, 0.354]. Under the pre-declared rule (CI entirely below zero = supported; at/above zero = refuted; straddling = inconclusive) the verdict is **inconclusive**. 40% of runs were censored at the step limit. The paper's contribution is a calibrated inconclusive measurement, a censoring-aware test design with explicit resolution limits, and an inventory of what the evidence cannot say.\nscripts/derive_resolution.py:44:    verdict = \"refuted\"\nscripts/derive_resolution.py:49:    \"verdict_from_predeclared_rule\": verdict,\npaper_strategy.json:6:  \"central_thesis\": \"Under a strictly specified and evaluator-owned protocol, the log-delay vs log2-width slope is -0.020 with a 95% seed-cluster bootstrap CI of [-0.585, 0.354] (4 seeds x 5 widths, 40% of runs censored at the step limit), so the 'delay decreases with width' prediction is neither supported nor refuted; the paper's contribution is a calibrated negative/inconclusive measurement, a censoring-aware test design whose resolution limits are made explicit, and a clear list of what the evidence cannot say.\",\npaper_strategy.json:7:  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The sealed final evaluation completed with outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome, status completed, correct true), so a paper about the measured results is supported. But 'qualified' certifies that the evaluation ran validly; it does not say the width hypothesis was supported. The decision rule fixed by the task contract (CI entirely below zero = supported, at/above zero = refuted, straddling = inconclusive) yields 'inconclusive' because the CI straddles zero. That is the most defensible and most honest story: a measurement-resolution paper. Alternatives rejected: (a) capacity-crossover / critical-width story (P1-P3 of the first model variant) -- no t_mem, t_gen, width-by-fraction or d*(f) results exist in the grounded artifacts; (b) lazy-to-rich or effective-learning-rate confound explanation -- the hidden-layer reparametrization sweep script was written but never executed (no code executed in that stage), so no evidence compares reparametrization strengths; (c) 'best search score = winner' story -- scores come from a different data plane, 2 seeds, and an undisclosed score formula; they cannot support a width claim. The retained claim is therefore narrow: what the final test measured and how little it resolves.\",\npaper_strategy.json:36:    \"Primary: final slope of ln(delay) vs log2(width) = -0.0198 with seed-cluster bootstrap 95% CI [-0.5846, 0.3542] (2000 resamples, 4 seeds, 5 widths 16-256), decision = inconclusive under the pre-declared rule; evaluator reference slope -0.3676 (single seed) lies inside the CI.\",\npaper_strategy.json:44:    \"Fig 1 (overview, early, page 1-2): concept schematic of the evaluation protocol and decision rule: width list -> train one-layer transformer at fixed AdamW settings -> checkpoint probes every 250 steps -> delay gap (first >=99% val minus first >=99% train, floored at 250, censored at 10,000) -> OLS slope of ln gap vs log2 width -> seed-cluster bootstrap CI -> three-way verdict (CI<0 supported / straddles 0 inconclusive / >=0 refuted); highlight where this run's CI lands.\",\npaper_strategy.json:69:      \"purpose\": \"Show the central result: slope CI straddles zero, reference slope inside CI, decision regions and attainable-slope bound\",\npaper_strategy.json:186:    \"Final outcome is 'qualified' with status completed (final.qualification.*). Say what that means narrowly (valid, completed evaluation); do not let it imply the width hypothesis was supported. The hypothesis verdict is inconclusive because the CI straddles zero. Gate diagnostics beyond the listed final.qualification.* values are unknown; say so rather than speculating.\",\npaper_strategy.json:195:    \"Body-page use (8-9 pages, ICLR single column): ~1.2 pages intro+question+contribution list; ~0.8 related work; ~2 pages protocol and model variant (delay definition, censoring, estimator, decision rule, frozen model constants); ~2 pages results (main slope figure, censoring and resolution analysis, validity checks, reference comparison); ~1 page discussion of what could and could not be concluded, alternative explanations (censoring ceiling, 4 seeds, reparametrization confound, single operation/prime); ~0.7 page limitations and scope. Fill with analysis, not padding; if the body is short, add the resolution-bound analysis and a descriptive seed-level table in main text rather than repeating text.\",\npaper_strategy.json:196:    \"Contributions must be verifiable: (1) a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with seed-cluster bootstrap and explicit three-way verdict; (2) a completed measurement on mod-97 addition with the stated outcome; (3) a derived resolution bound explaining why the test is weak here; (4) an explicit inventory of untested explanations.\",\nderived/resolution.json:2: \"verdict_from_predeclared_rule\": \"inconclusive\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"s