 design whose resolution limits are made explicit, and a clear list of what the evidence cannot say.\",\npaper_strategy.json:7:  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The sealed final evaluation completed with outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome, status completed, correct true), so a paper about the measured results is supported. But 'qualified' certifies that the evaluation ran validly; it does not say the width hypothesis was supported. The decision rule fixed by the task contract (CI entirely below zero = supported, at/above zero = refuted, straddling = inconclusive) yields 'inconclusive' because the CI straddles zero. That is the most defensible and most honest story: a measurement-resolution paper. Alternatives rejected: (a) capacity-crossover / critical-width story (P1-P3 of the first model variant) -- no t_mem, t_gen, width-by-fraction or d*(f) results exist in the grounded artifacts; (b) lazy-to-rich or effective-learning-rate confound explanation -- the hidden-layer reparametrization sweep script was written but never executed (no code executed in that stage), so no evidence compares reparametrization strengths; (c) 'best search score = winner' story -- scores come from a different data plane, 2 seeds, and an undisclosed score formula; they cannot support a width claim. The retained claim is therefore narrow: what the final test measured and how little it resolves.\",\npaper_strategy.json:36:    \"Primary: final slope of ln(delay) vs log2(width) = -0.0198 with seed-cluster bootstrap 95% CI [-0.5846, 0.3542] (2000 resamples, 4 seeds, 5 widths 16-256), decision = inconclusive under the pre-declared rule; evaluator reference slope -0.3676 (single seed) lies inside the CI.\",\npaper_strategy.json:44:    \"Fig 1 (overview, early, page 1-2): concept schematic of the evaluation protocol and decision rule: width list -> train one-layer transformer at fixed AdamW settings -> checkpoint probes every 250 steps -> delay gap (first >=99% val minus first >=99% train, floored at 250, censored at 10,000) -> OLS slope of ln gap vs log2 width -> seed-cluster bootstrap CI -> three-way verdict (CI<0 supported / straddles 0 inconclusive / >=0 refuted); highlight where this run's CI lands.\",\npaper_strategy.json:69:      \"purpose\": \"Show the central result: slope CI straddles zero, reference slope inside CI, decision regions and attainable-slope bound\",\npaper_strategy.json:186:    \"Final outcome is 'qualified' with status completed (final.qualification.*). Say what that means narrowly (valid, completed evaluation); do not let it imply the width hypothesis was supported. The hypothesis verdict is inconclusive because the CI straddles zero. Gate diagnostics beyond the listed final.qualification.* values are unknown; say so rather than speculating.\",\npaper_strategy.json:195:    \"Body-page use (8-9 pages, ICLR single column): ~1.2 pages intro+question+contribution list; ~0.8 related work; ~2 pages protocol and model variant (delay definition, censoring, estimator, decision rule, frozen model constants); ~2 pages results (main slope figure, censoring and resolution analysis, validity checks, reference comparison); ~1 page discussion of what could and could not be concluded, alternative explanations (censoring ceiling, 4 seeds, reparametrization confound, single operation/prime); ~0.7 page limitations and scope. Fill with analysis, not padding; if the body is short, add the resolution-bound analysis and a descriptive seed-level table in main text rather than repeating text.\",\npaper_strategy.json:196:    \"Contributions must be verifiable: (1) a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with seed-cluster bootstrap and explicit three-way verdict; (2) a completed measurement on mod-97 addition with the stated outcome; (3) a derived resolution bound explaining why the test is weak here; (4) an explicit inventory of untested explanations.\",\nderived/resolution.json:2: \"verdict_from_predeclared_rule\": \"inconclusive\",","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
